# -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-
"""Soundex Codec - Soundex phonetic content encoding.

Soundex, patented by Robert C. Russell and Margaret K. Odell in 1918, indexes names by their sound, as used by the US
 National Archives for the censuses: each word is written as its first letter followed by three digits for its next
 consonants (B, F, P and V give 1 ; C, G, J, K, Q, S, X and Z give 2 ; D and T give 3 ; L gives 4 ; M and N give 5 ;
 R gives 6), the vowels (and Y) being dropped, the consecutive consonants with the same digit (including the first
 letter, or separated by H or W) giving a single digit, and the code being padded with zeros or truncated (e.g.
 "Robert" and "Rupert" give "R163", "Pfister" gives "P236" and "Ashcraft" gives "A261").

Encoding replaces each word by its code (accented letters being written without their accent), keeping the other
 characters. As Soundex is not reversible, decoding replaces each code by the most frequent word of an English word list
 with this code (9850 words, from the lists of Ciphey), or, with the "-all" variant, by all these words in order of
 frequency (e.g. "[robert|rupert]").

This codec:
- en/decodes strings from str to str
- en/decodes strings from bytes to bytes
- decodes file content to str (read)
- encodes file content from str to bytes (write)

References: https://www.archives.gov/research/census/soundex
            https://github.com/Ciphey/CipheyDists (word lists, released into the public domain)
"""
